.TH GENDEF 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
gendef \- write a module-definition file from the exports of a DLL
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gendef
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-I
.IR dir ]...
.RB [ \-\-no\-include\-current\-dir ]
.RB [ \- ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gendef
reads the export table of each
.IR file ,
a PE image such as a DLL, and writes a module-definition (DEF) file that
lists its exports, from which
.BR dlltool (1)
makes an import library.
It is gendef 1.1, from Mingw-w64 14.0.0.
It reads 32-bit (PE32) and 64-bit (PE32+) images of any machine type.
In a 32-bit x86 image it also guesses the stdcall decoration of each
function, which the export table lacks.
.PP
For each
.IR file ,
.B gendef
writes a DEF file in the current directory, named for the part of
.I file
after its last slash or backslash, with its final extension, if any,
replaced by
.BR .def ,
so
.I lib/foo.dll
gives
.IR foo.def .
An existing file of that name is replaced.
With
.BR \- ,
it writes every DEF file to standard output instead.
A file without exports produces no output and no message.
.SS Output format
A DEF file begins with
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
LIBRARY "\fIname\fR"
EXPORTS
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I name
is the DLL name recorded in the export table, which may differ from the
file name.
A line for each export follows, in ordinal order, with these parts in
this order, each but the first only where it applies:
.TP
.I name
The export name, in double quotes when it contains a dot.
An export without a name is
.BI ord_ n\fR,
where
.I n
is its ordinal.
.TP
.BI @ size
The argument size in bytes of a stdcall function in a 32-bit x86 image,
as described under
.B Decorations
below.
.TP
.BI "= " target
For a forwarded export, the export it forwards to, as
.IB dll . name
or
.IB dll .# ordinal\fR.
.TP
.BI @ ordinal " NONAME"
For an export without a name.
Named exports are written without their ordinals, so an import library
made from the file imports them by name.
.TP
.B DATA
For an export that is data rather than a function.
.TP
.B PRIVATE
For these names, which programs do not import, so that an import library
leaves them out:
.BR DllCanUnloadNow ,
.BR DllEntryPoint ,
.BR DllGetClassFactoryFromClassString ,
.BR DllGetClassObject ,
.BR DllGetDocumentation ,
.BR DllInitialize ,
.BR DllInstall ,
.BR DllMain ,
.BR DllRegisterServer ,
.BR DllRegisterServerEx ,
.BR DllRegisterServerExW ,
.BR DllUnload ,
.BR DllUnregisterServer ,
.BR RasCustomDeleteEntryNotify ,
.BR RasCustomDial ,
.BR RasCustomDialDlg ,
and
.BR RasCustomEntryDlg .
.TP
.BI "== " name
The name as the DLL exports it, for some decorated names, as described
under
.B Decorations
below.
.TP
.BI "; " comment
A warning about a guess, as described under
.B Comments
below.
.PP
Lines end with CRLF.
.SS Data exports
An export is
.B DATA
when any of these hold:
.IP \(bu 2
Its address lies in a section that is not executable and is readable or
discardable, such as
.BR .data ,
.BR .rdata ,
or
.BR .bss .
.IP \(bu 2
A base relocation applies at its address, so that it holds a pointer.
.IP \(bu 2
Its name begins with
.BR ??_7 ,
as an MSVC virtual function table does.
.IP \(bu 2
In a 32-bit x86 image, its first instruction is one that
.B gendef
does not recognize, which wrongly marks some functions as data.
.IP \(bu 2
A hint file marks it
.BR DATA .
.SS Decorations
In a 32-bit x86 image,
.B gendef
writes the argument size of each stdcall function as a decoration,
.BI @ n\fR,
after its name, as in
.BR mul@8 .
.BR dlltool (1)
with
.B \-k
then makes an import library whose symbols carry the decoration, as the
compiler references them, but that imports the names without it.
.PP
To find the size,
.B gendef
decodes the function's machine code from its entry point.
It follows unconditional jumps and steps over calls, and when a path
ends without a return, as at an indirect jump, it tries the targets of
the conditional jumps it passed.
The first return instruction it reaches decides:
.BI "ret " n
gives the size
.IR n ,
and a plain
.BR ret ,
which both cdecl functions and stdcall functions without arguments use,
leaves the size unknown, unless given
.BR \-a .
Since calls are stepped over, after a call to a function that does not
return, the search runs on into whatever code follows.
When the size is still unknown and the code jumps through the import
address table to an imported function, the size of that function is
looked up in a hint file.
.PP
The size is written as follows, with an unknown size taken as 0 in the
comparisons:
.IP \(bu 2
A name of the form
.BI _ name @ n\fR,
where
.I n
is the size, loses its underscore and is followed by
.BI "== _" name @ n\fR,
as in
.BR "mul@8 == _mul@8" .
.IP \(bu 2
A fastcall name,
.BI @ name @ m\fR,
is kept and followed by
.BI "== @" name @ m
when
.I m
is the size plus 8, or when the size is 0 and
.I m
is 0, 4, or 8.
.IP \(bu 2
A C++ name, which begins with
.BR ? ,
is kept, and a known size goes in a comment.
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise a known size is appended, even to a name that already has a
decoration, so
.B mul@8
becomes
.BR mul@8@8 .
.PP
Other images have no decorations: names are written as exported.
.SS Hint files
For each forwarded export, and, in a 32-bit x86 image, for each function
whose size is still unknown and that jumps to an imported function,
.B gendef
looks up the function forwarded to or imported in a hint file.
That is a DEF file for the other DLL, named for that DLL's name up to
its first dot, in lowercase, plus
.BR .def .
For an import from
.I KERNEL32.dll
or a forward to
.BR KERNEL32.GetTickCount ,
it is
.IR kernel32.def .
A forward to an ordinal, as in
.BR KERNEL32.#100 ,
looks up the name
.BR ord_100 ,
but a function imported by ordinal is looked up under the part of its
DLL's name after the first dot, such as
.BR dll .
.PP
Hint files are looked for in the current directory, unless given
.BR \-\-no\-include\-current\-dir ,
then in each
.B \-I
directory, the last given first.
Since
.B gendef
writes its DEF files in the current directory, one written earlier in a
run serves as a hint file for the files after it.
Each hint file is read at most once per run, so one that
.B gendef
replaces after reading it keeps its old contents for the rest of the
run.
.PP
A line of a hint file matches when it begins with the function's name
followed by
.BR @ ,
white space, or the end of the line.
The digits right after that character give the size, and
.B DATA
anywhere later in the line marks the export as data.
A name without a decoration gives 0, including one that
.B gendef
wrote for a function whose size it could not find.
In a file with LF line endings, a name alone on its line takes its size
and
.B DATA
from the next line.
The first line of a hint file never matches, and neither does a line
that begins with white space.
Outside 32-bit x86 images, only
.B DATA
is used.
.SS Comments
A comment at the end of an export's line flags a guess:
.IP \(bu 2
.BI "; has WINAPI (@" n )
follows a C++ name whose function has the size
.IR n .
.IP \(bu 2
.BI "; Check!!! return value is from " name " in " dll\fR,
or
.BI "; Check!!! return value is from ord " n " in " dll\fR,
follows a function that jumps to a function imported from
.I dll
by name or by ordinal, when no hint file gave the size.
Despite the wording, it is the argument size that is unknown.
.IP \(bu 2
.BI "; Check!!! forwards to " target
follows a forwarded export that is not data and either has no name or,
in a 32-bit x86 image, has an unknown size.
It is written even with
.BR \-f .
.IP \(bu 2
.B "; Check!!! Couldn\(aqt determine function argument count.\&"
.B "Function doesn\(aqt return.\&"
follows a function in which no return instruction was found.
.SH OPTIONS
Each option is a separate argument.
Options cannot be grouped, an option's argument is the next argument,
and the long options take no
.BR = .
Options apply to every file, wherever they appear.
Any other argument is a file, including an unknown option and
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.B \-
Write the DEF files to standard output, one after another, instead of
to files.
Standard input cannot be read.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard error and exit without reading any
file.
With no arguments,
.B gendef
does the same.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-assume\-stdcall
In a 32-bit x86 image, take a function that returns with a plain
.B ret
to be stdcall without arguments, and decorate it with
.BR @0 .
Since cdecl functions return the same way, they get
.B @0
too, so this suits a DLL whose functions are all stdcall, such as a
Windows system DLL.
.TP
\fB\-I \fIdir\fR, \fB\-\-include\-def\-path \fIdir\fR
Look for hint files in
.IR dir .
As the last argument,
.B \-I
prints a message on standard error, without a newline, and is otherwise
ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-include\-current\-dir
Do not look for hint files in the current directory.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-no\-forward\-output
Omit the
.BI "= " target
part of forwarded exports, which are still listed.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-disable\-fs\-redirector
Disable WOW64 file system redirection, so that a 32-bit
.B gendef
on 64-bit Windows reads the 64-bit DLLs in
.I system32
instead of the 32-bit DLLs in
.IR SysWOW64 .
Where there is no redirection to disable, as in the 64-bit kit, it
prints a message on standard error and has no other effect.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, and also when a file cannot be read or is not a PE image, or
its DEF file cannot be written.
.PP
Problems are reported on standard error, and
.B gendef
goes on to the next file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
*** [\fIfile\fR] failed to open()
*** [\fIfile\fR] unable to allocate \fIsize\fR bytes
*** [\fIfile\fR] not a PE(+) file
*** [\fIfile\fR] no PE(+) signature
*** [\fIfile\fR] no PE(+) optional header
 * failed to create \fIdeffile\fR ...
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I file
is the argument and
.I deffile
the name of its DEF file.
The second is also reported for an empty file and for a read error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write
.I foo.def
for
.IR foo.dll :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ gendef foo.dll
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the DEF file of a 32-bit DLL, with a stdcall function
.B mul
that takes two
.B int
arguments, and a cdecl function
.BR add :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ gendef \- demo.dll
LIBRARY "demo.dll"
EXPORTS
DllMain@12 PRIVATE
add
counter DATA
mul@8
ord_9 @9 NONAME
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make an import library for a 64-bit DLL:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ gendef foo.dll
$ dlltool \-d foo.def \-l libfoo.a
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make an import library for that 32-bit DLL in the 64-bit kit, with
.B \-k
to remove the decorations from the imported names:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ gendef demo.dll
$ i686\-w64\-mingw32\-dlltool \-k \-d demo.def \-l libdemo.a
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write DEF files for two 32-bit DLLs, where
.I wrapper.dll
forwards
.B plus
to
.B add
in
.IR base.dll ,
so that
.IR base.def ,
written first, gives the size of the forwarded function:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ gendef base.dll wrapper.dll
$ cat wrapper.def
LIBRARY "wrapper.dll"
EXPORTS
plus@8 = base.add
twice@8
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The kit also installs
.B gendef
under its target-prefixed name, such as
.BR x86_64\-w64\-mingw32\-gendef ,
and the 64-bit kit adds
.BR i686\-w64\-mingw32\-gendef .
Each runs the same program, with no added options, and reads 32-bit and
64-bit images alike.
.PP
Unlike upstream gendef, the kit's build starts each DEF file at
.BR LIBRARY ,
without a comment header, which upstream uses to say when the file needs
.BR "dlltool \-k" ,
and prints no progress message for each image.
.PP
.BR dlltool (1)
cannot read a forward to an ordinal, as in
.BR "= KERNEL32.#100" :
it reports a syntax error and leaves that export and every later one out
of the import library, so use
.B \-f
for such a DLL.
It reads the decoration of a name in quotes, as in
.BR \(dqa.b\(dq@4 ,
as an ordinal, so the decoration is lost.
.PP
When several names share one ordinal, only the last of them in the
image's name table, which is sorted, is written.
.PP
When a DEF file cannot be created, the exports of that image are written
at the start of the next DEF file instead, with their decorations
guessed from the wrong image, which can also mark them as data.
.PP
A drive prefix stays in the output name:
.I d:foo.dll
gives
.IR d:foo.def ,
in the current directory of drive D.
.PP
Paths must be representable in the ANSI code page.
Each file is read whole into memory.
.B gendef
trusts the offsets in an image, so a malformed image can crash it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dlltool (1),
.BR ld (1),
.BR objdump (1),
.BR peports (1)
